  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure builds in briefly tonight into early Wednesday \nwith patchy to localized areas of radiational mist or fog \noccurring across the region, especially in the sheltered\nvalleys, as temps fall off into the 50s to around 60F with a \nfew upper 40s over the southwest Adirondacks. The mist or fog \nburns off quickly with mid and high clouds increasing ahead of a\npre-frontal sfc trough and a cold front approaching from the \nGreat Lakes Region and southeast Canada. The best chance of \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will be west/northwest of \nthe Greater Capital Region. The latest HREFs indicates mean \nSBCAPE of 250-750 J/kg with 0-6 km shear 30-40 KT. Some of the \nCAMs like the 3-km NAM have SBCAPEs around 1000 J/kg or so with \nhigher dewpts. The instability being limited showed keep \nupdrafts from being tall enough for any severe threat. Some \ngusty winds may occur with a few storms...and the showers \nweaken, as they move towards the Capital District and Hudson \nValley by nightfall. Max temps rise into the lower to mid 80s in\nthe valleys and upper to 70s over the hills and mtns. \n\nThe pre-frontal trough moves through at night with the showers\nweakening, as they move eastward with limited upper level \nsupport and moisture convergence. The cold front will be moving \nacross the southern half of the forecast area in the late \nmorning through the afternoon. Some patchy fog may occur \nespecially north and west of the Albany in the morning. The \nlatest NBM had very little PoPs north of the mid-Hudson Valley. \nWe collaborated with WFOs GYX/BTV/BOX and expanded the slight \nchance of showers to southern VT, the southeast Capital Region \nand Berkshires and points south. If the front is faster, then \nthe showers and isolated thunderstorms will be south of the Mass\nPike and closer to I-84. Once again, instability looks limited \nwith SBCAPEs mainly 750 J/kg or less near I-84 in southern \nDutchess and Litchfield Counties, so thunderstorms are expected \nto be non-severe. High pressure builds in Thu night thru Friday\nwith cooler and drier weather. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next chance of a widespread rainfall looks to be late\nSaturday into Sunday. Mid and upper-level height falls occur\nover the Great Lakes Region, southeast Canada into the\nNortheast, as a deep upper trough develops based on the medium \nrange deterministic, AI and ensemble guidance. A series of \nshort-waves dig into the trough, as it amplifies and may close \noff somewhere near NY and New England Sun into Mon. It may be as\nfar poleward as near James Bay. A southern stream disturbance or\nsfc low may form near the Mid Atlantic Coast with the \ndeveloping upper-level trough Sat night into Sunday bringing \nperiods of rainfall with a few thunderstorms. The latest 13Z/NBM\nindicates the probabilities >0.50\" of rainfall for the 24-hr \nperiod ending 8 pm Sunday are 40-70% across eastern NY and \nwestern New England. The probs for >1.00\" of rainfall are only \n20-40%. The placement of the closed low and the amount of QPF \nare still somewhat uncertain, but some locally heavy rainfall \nmay occur Sun-Mon.  We will continue to monitor guidance trends\nthroughout the week. Temps trend cooler than normal for the 2nd\nhalf of the weekend into early next week. A break in the pcpn \nmay occur Mon night into Tue, as the upper low moves down \nstream.",
